---
export const prerender = true;
import EditorialArticleLayout from '../../components/EditorialArticleLayout.astro';

const slug = 'tesseract-js-browser-ocr-image-quality';
const title = 'How to Improve Tesseract.js OCR Results in a Browser';
const description =
  'Prepare clearer images for Tesseract.js OCR, choose the matching language, check important characters, and try the verified English sample workflow.';
const imagePath = '/social/tesseract-js-browser-ocr-image-quality.webp';
const socialImagePath = '/social/tesseract-js-browser-ocr-image-quality.png';
const imageAlt =
  'Full-body smoke-kawaii girl checking document image quality beside a laptop, magnifying glass, glare panel, and crop marks.';
const sources = [
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js', label: 'Tesseract.js official repository' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/api.md', label: 'Tesseract.js worker API and image-resolution guidance' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/local-installation.md', label: 'Tesseract.js worker, core, and language-file paths' },
  { href: 'https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html', label: 'Tesseract OCR image-quality guide' },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/', label: 'Image to Text OCR Tool', description: 'Choose one supported image and a language, then check and copy the recognized text.' },
  { href: '/blog/how-to-use-image-to-text-ocr-tool/', label: 'Image to Text OCR guide', description: 'Download the sample and follow the actual tool controls and limits.' },
  { href: '/blog/browser-ai-vs-local-ai-privacy/', label: 'Browser AI vs local AI', description: 'See what browser processing does and does not establish about privacy.' },
  { href: '/categories/ai-tools/', label: 'Browser AI tools', description: 'Explore other browser helpers with stated model and accuracy limits.' },
];
---

<EditorialArticleLayout
  slug={slug}
  title={title}
  seoTitle="Improve Tesseract.js OCR Results in a Browser"
  description={description}
  label="Browser OCR guide"
  breadcrumbLabel="Improve Tesseract.js browser OCR"
  imagePath={imagePath}
  socialImagePath={socialImagePath}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  imageCaption="The mascot checks lighting, text size, cropping, and language choice before copying recognized text."
  sources={sources}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A copied receipt total can look plausible while a decimal point or digit is wrong. OCR gives
      you a text draft to check against the image. This guide explains how to prepare that image,
      use the browser tool, and check the result before trusting it.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Quick answer</h2>
    <p>
      Keep complete characters, readable text size, even lighting, and straight text lines. Choose
      the main language in the image. Compare names, totals, dates, codes, and punctuation with the
      original before copying them into another document. A confidence percentage does not verify
      each character.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/">Image to Text OCR Tool</a> accepts one still PNG,
      JPEG, or WebP at a time. It has no crop, rotate, contrast, or sharpening controls. Prepare a
      copy in your image editor, or retake the photograph, before selecting it here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Fix the source image before applying filters</h2>
    <p>
      If a bright reflection covers a price or account code, move the camera, document, or light and
      take another image. Sharpening cannot recover characters that the photo never captured. Keep
      the original so you can compare it with any edited copy.
    </p>
    <p>
      For tiny text, use a larger original or a closer, focused photo. Enlarging an existing image
      may help recognition, but it cannot restore missing detail. Change one condition at a time,
      then compare the same line of output. That makes it easier to see whether the edit helped.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Tesseract.js's <a href="https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/api.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">worker API</a>
      recommends sufficiently high-resolution input and notes that upscaling can improve results.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Keep a border, and straighten the lines</h2>
    <p>
      Crop unnecessary surroundings without cutting through letters. Leave a small margin around
      the text. Rotate a tilted image until its lines are horizontal. Check the first and last
      character after saving: a neat crop can still remove part of a reference number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tesseract's quality guide covers uneven backgrounds, noise, rotation, borders, and resolution.
      Its 300 dpi recommendation is scan guidance, not a pass threshold for this browser tool. The
      tool checks image dimensions and byte size; it does not judge whether your text is readable.
      A heavy filter can erase punctuation, so compare edited and original images closely.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Background: <a href="https://tesseract-ocr.github.io/tessdoc/ImproveQuality.html" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Tesseract's image-quality guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Choose the language, then check the words</h2>
    <p>
      This tool offers English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, and Portuguese. Select the main
      language yourself; it does not automatically detect it. For a Spanish document, choose Spanish
      and check accented words, names, and any foreign-language text in the result. A matching
      language file is not a guarantee that every accent or proper noun will be right.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep a short checklist of fields that matter. On a receipt, compare the currency sign, decimal
      separator, total, date, and reference number. On a form, compare names and identifiers. Keep
      leading zeros in codes. If the output and image disagree, correct the text from the image;
      do not use a high confidence percentage to settle the disagreement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Try one checked English example</h2>
    <p>
      Download the <a href="/samples/ocr-synthetic-english.png" download="ocr-synthetic-english.png">synthetic English sample</a>.
      It is a 1,200 by 220 pixel PNG with black 64px Arial text on a white background. Its reference
      line is <code>ACCESS FREE TOOLS 12345</code>. This simple image helps you check the image selection,
      recognition, and copy controls without using a private document.
    </p>
    <ol class="editorial-checklist">
      <li>Open the <a href="/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/">OCR tool</a> and select the downloaded PNG.</li>
      <li>Choose English, then press <strong>Read text</strong>.</li>
      <li>Compare the result with <code>ACCESS FREE TOOLS 12345</code>.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Copy result</strong> and paste it into a text editor to check the copied line.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      An automated Chromium check on October 5, 2026 used the real tool component and its local
      Tesseract.js 7.0.0 assets. It recognized that exact line and copied the same line to the browser
      clipboard. The check blocked external requests. It establishes this one simple English
      workflow, not a measured accuracy result for blur, glare, receipts, Spanish, or other devices.
    </p>
    <p>
      For your own document, keep the source image and compare the text before and after an edit.
      Record the selected language and any changes to the image. A few successful lines are useful
      checks; they do not establish a general OCR accuracy rate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>What the browser downloads and processes</h2>
    <p>
      The current OCR component prepares the selected image and sends it to a worker inside the
      browser for recognition. Access Free Tools serves the worker, WebAssembly core, and selected
      language files from its own model directory after you press <strong>Read text</strong>. The
      recognition path does not upload the image to the site server.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tesseract.js supports explicit worker, core, and language-file paths. This tool uses those local
      paths rather than the default external downloads. The page still makes support-file requests,
      and the site has separate analytics and session replay. A tool-usage event is not the image or
      extracted text, but local recognition alone does not establish anonymity for the whole page.
      Read the <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a> before handling sensitive documents.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Background: <a href="https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js/blob/master/docs/local-installation.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Tesseract.js's local installation guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>When the result needs another check</h2>
    <p>
      The current input limit is 10 MiB, with at most eight million pixels and neither side above
      8,192 pixels. Resize a copy if needed. This is a one-image text extractor, not a multipage PDF
      reader, layout-preserving converter, or verified handwriting service.
    </p>
    <p>
      A different font, camera, language, browser, or Tesseract version can change recognition.
      Verify important financial, medical, legal, or identity text directly against its source.
      The <a href="/blog/how-to-use-image-to-text-ocr-tool/">full OCR guide</a> explains the supported
      inputs and controls. Use the <a href="/tools/image-to-text-ocr-tool/">OCR tool</a> to save
      typing, then check the draft before it becomes a record you rely on.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialArticleLayout>
